Throughout this module, I explored several Windows system administration tools available through System Configuration (MSConfig) 
and learned how they can be used to manage, monitor, and troubleshoot a computer.

I worked with Advanced System Settings and User Account Control (UAC), understanding how Windows manages system configurations and user permissions. 
I also explored Computer Management, including its administrative tools, and System Information (msinfo32), which provides detailed information about hardware,
system components, and software environments.

Another important part of this module was learning how to use Resource Monitor (resmon.exe) to observe system performance and resource usage.
I also practiced using the Command Prompt, working with commands such as hostname, whoami, ipconfig, ipconfig /all, and netstat to retrieve system and network information.

Finally, I explored the Windows Registry through Registry Editor (regedit), learning about its role in storing system, hardware, and software configurations,
as well as the importance of being careful when making changes.

This module helped me develop a better understanding of Windows administration, system monitoring, troubleshooting, and configuration management. 
These skills are important in Cybersecurity, especially when analyzing systems, identifying configuration issues, and understanding how operating systems work.
